INSERT INTO `submissions` VALUES (9,67,'submissions/2/259/1764952408_index.zip','1764952408_index.zip',1,'graded','Excellent work! The HTML structure is well-organized and uses semantic tags appropriately. The use of `<header>`, `<main>`, `<article>`, `<section>`, and `<footer>` significantly improves the document\'s accessibility and SEO. The inclusion of Tailwind CSS makes the styling consistent and readable. The comments explaining the purpose of each section are helpful. There are just a few minor suggestions for improvement:\n\n1.  Consider adding an `aria-label` attribute to the `<nav>` element to provide a more descriptive label for screen readers, especially if there are multiple navigation sections on a more complex page. For example: `<nav aria-label=\"Main Navigation\">`\n2.  While Tailwind CSS is convenient for rapid prototyping and small projects, in larger projects, it can lead to larger CSS bundles and potential performance issues. Be mindful of this trade-off as projects grow. Consider purging unused styles in production to minimize CSS file size.\n3.  The inline comments explaining the semantic tags are a good start, but strive for self-documenting code as much as possible. Clean semantic structure often speaks for itself. These comments might become outdated as the project evolves.\n\nOverall, this is a very well-structured and readable HTML document.',92,'2025-12-05 08:33:28','2025-12-05 08:33:31'),(25,73,'submissions/1/259/1767078765_testing2.zip','1767078765_testing2.zip',1,'graded','## Grading Report: HTML Structure\n\n**Score:** 85/100\n\n### Summary\nThe submission demonstrates a solid understanding of the core concepts required for \'HTML Structure\'. The code is well-structured and follows standard conventions. However, there are a few areas where improvements can be made, particularly regarding error handling and inline documentation.\n\n### Detailed Feedback\n\n#### Strengths\n- **Code Organization:** The project structure is logical and easy to navigate.\n- **Functionality:** The core requirements appear to be met based on the code analysis.\n- **Syntax:** No major syntax errors were detected.\n\n#### Areas for Improvement\n- **Error Handling:** Consider adding more robust try-catch blocks around external API calls or database operations.\n- **Comments:** While the code is readable, adding DocBlocks to complex methods would enhance maintainability.\n- **Input Validation:** Ensure all user inputs are validated before processing to prevent security vulnerabilities.\n\n### Recommendation\nReview the error handling logic and add comments to the key functions. Great job on the initial implementation!',85,'2025-12-29 23:12:45','2025-12-29 23:12:45'),(26,25,'submissions/1/19/1767079031_testing2.zip','1767079031_testing2.zip',1,'graded','## Grading Report: Frontend Setup\n\n**Score:** 85/100\n\n### Summary\nThe submission demonstrates a solid understanding of the core concepts required for \'Frontend Setup\'. The code is well-structured and follows standard conventions. However, there are a few areas where improvements can be made, particularly regarding error handling and inline documentation.\n\n### Detailed Feedback\n\n#### Strengths\n- **Code Organization:** The project structure is logical and easy to navigate.\n- **Functionality:** The core requirements appear to be met based on the code analysis.\n- **Syntax:** No major syntax errors were detected.\n\n#### Areas for Improvement\n- **Error Handling:** Consider adding more robust try-catch blocks around external API calls or database operations.\n- **Comments:** While the code is readable, adding DocBlocks to complex methods would enhance maintainability.\n- **Input Validation:** Ensure all user inputs are validated before processing to prevent security vulnerabilities.\n\n### Recommendation\nReview the error handling logic and add comments to the key functions. Great job on the initial implementation!',85,'2025-12-29 23:17:11','2025-12-29 23:17:11'),